    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d302-a1">

                <pb facs="0408" n="340" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg340"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure 1—Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The German Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bernstorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the German Foreign Office
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <p>No. 48. I have informed Mr. Lansing of contents of your wireless No. 29.<anchor
                        n="17" xml:id="d302-f1"/> He has not yet received reply from our enemies
                    concerning his proposal to disarm merchant vessels. Mr. Lansing does not wish to
                    give me copy of his note till he has received an answer which he expects will be
                    refusal. Mr. Lansing reminded me of fact that from the beginning of controversy
                    with us the American Government always spoke of unarmed merchant vessels
                    (American note of May 13th 1915<anchor n="18" xml:id="d302-f2"/>). As to
                    objections of Mr. Lansing to the word “welcome” I refer to Baron Zwiedineks
                    wireless No. 52.<anchor n="19" xml:id="d302-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>[File copy not signed]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
